  "lineage": "Sacred Hawaiian chant (oli) performed in the low, bent-knee haʻa posture reserved for akua and ancestors, encompassing genealogical (koʻihonua) and name (mele inoa) chants that transmit lineage and cosmology. The style is marked by sustained, measured vocal delivery and stylized ornaments such as the ʻiʻi trill, and was traditionally taught within the hula hālau under a kumu hula. The corpus and its performance practice were documented in Nathaniel B. Emerson's Unwritten Literature of Hawaii (1909) and in the later scholarship of Mary Kawena Pukui. Chant carried genealogy, prayer, and place-lore as authoritative oral record in pre-literate Hawaiʻi.",
